It was surprisingly hard to find a game design principles article without being some sort of Top 10 list. The game design principles I've chosen to talk about is to design the game around a core mechanic, reward, competition, and sound design. Sound design is used everywhere, for me at least it is the most important part of a game aside from actual mechanics. Sound design can be a clear indication for an action or event happening such as landing an attack, blocking, movement, etc. Ambiance added by the sound adds more immersion for the player which allows some players to enjoy themselves more during gameplay. Reward is used to make sure the player receives an incentive for the challenges they overcame, like getting something from a chest. Core mechanic is important in giving a game its identity and how to approach the game. For example, the core mechanic in Mario is jumping, shooting in Call of Duty, and mining in minecraft.

The cons with the game design principles I've listed mainly comes down to how you use it. If rewards are not given out in proportion to the challenge it can lead to player frustration. If competition can be curbed by, let's say microtransaction, then players will be frustrated that someone threw their wallet at the game and is ranked higher or becomes stronger than them. Sound design when not used well can create frustrating gameplay interactions or make an interaction not feel as impactful as it should be. Core mechanics when not expanded well enough feels repetitive to the players.

I am most likely not going to be competition because I want to do a 2d platformer, maybe a leaderboard for completion time but that would be like way later down the line if I get that far, definitely not in this quarter. I think it could be interesting to create a 2D platformer that has ranked but I don't know how well it would be. Sound design I will do for sure I just don't know how indepth, this is mainly because of our 10 week time constraint, I will probably just go with copyright free sounds or simple sounds. Reward I will definitely use as well like a chest. Core mechanic will for sure be used and focused on movement because the core gameplay of a 2D platformer, whether you have combat or not, is the way the player moves.

